        "id": 100116,
        "lang": "en",
        "title": "When Do Larger Batches Help Scale LLM Reinforcement Learning?",
        "desc": "Larger batches expose more parallelism by processing more sequences per update, but under a fixed sample budget, they also leave fewer opportunities to update the model. When does this trade-off reduce wall-clock time-to-target in reinforcement learning? For large language models, answering this question requires accounting for the online generation of training trajectories and the computational asymmetry between generation and training. We develop a framework that connects samples-to-target with end-to-end throughput: a larger batch accelerates training only when its throughput gain exceeds its samples-to-target penalty. This framework also suggests a simple practical strategy—first establish approximate batch-size invariance, then optimize throughput within that regime. Experiments with GRPO and PPO show that learning-rate retuning approximately preserves sample-indexed learning trajectories over a bounded batch range. On fixed hardware, larger batches improve generation throughput by up to 2.29x, and our best measured GRPO configuration reaches the same validation target in 29% less time without additional GPUs.",
        "descTitle": "Abstract",
        "descVisible": 1,
        "author": "RL Team",
